WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.335
NARRATOR: The cannibal
sharks investigation

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:03.570
heads to Bimini in the Bahamas.

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.744
The mangrove swamps here
are a precious nursery

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:11.544
for lemon sharks.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.550
Every year, scores
of pregnant females

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.319
return to these
shallow waters where

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:21.521
they were born to give birth.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:33.266
But in a Machiavellian twist
that surprised scientists,

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:38.304
someone was eating the kids.

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.573
TRISTAN GUTTRIDGE:
Of 100 newborn sharks

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.644
born in June of one year,
by June the next year,

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:46.546
around 50 are left.

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:50.417
So 50% of the
cohort in every year

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:52.819
are eaten or die
from starvation.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:59.926
NARRATOR: This pup is less
than two feet long, one

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:01.861
of a litter of up to 17 pups.

00:01:04.631 --> 00:01:06.666
Scientists from the
Bimini Shark Lab

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:12.105
have been studying the lemons
here for more than 20 years.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.541
They found the pups can
stay up to eight years

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.177
here, learning to
hunt and hide among

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.878
the twisted mangrove roots.

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:25.318
But the shock discovery
was the pups' biggest

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:28.755
threat comes from their own.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:32.125
TRISTAN GUTTRIDGE: Everything
seems to kick off at high tide.

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:35.361
As the water rises,
it basically gives

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:39.299
access to shallow water
areas for big predators

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.400
to come in as well.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.338
That's when these big
lemon sharks come in

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:48.341
and start patrolling and
cruising the outskirts

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:49.175
of those mangroves.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.944
[music playing]

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.694
NARRATOR: The young
are on high alert.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:13.132
They instinctively
hug the mangrove roots

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.101
which offer some protection.

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:24.043
But if they stray too
far, the hunt is on.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.379
TRISTAN GUTTRIDGE: For a baby
lemon shark that's in close

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:29.315
proximity to a
big adult, if they

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:33.486
haven't seen it before it's seen
them, they've got no chance.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:39.526
They can burst
attack very quickly

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:42.195
and ambush really effectively.

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:00.613
I've seen firsthand bite marks.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.082
I've seen adults
chasing juveniles.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.385
You know, we know
it's happening.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:08.121
NARRATOR: It's clear evidence
shark cannibalism goes on

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:10.290
in the wild.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:13.726
But why would you eat your own?

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.461
TRISTAN GUTTRIDGE: The
one great advantage

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:19.032
that an adult lemon shark has
is insider knowledge on where

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:21.034
the juveniles are
hiding and what

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.303
tactics they use
to avoid predators,

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.038
because it's been one itself.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.174
And that gives it
an extra advantage,

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:31.210
and probably why they then
pick off the juveniles

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.513
of their own species.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.082
What's happening here
in Bimini isn't unique.

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:39.485
Throughout the world
in other nursery areas,

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.554
big sharks are
eating little sharks.

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:48.661
It comes down to needing to eat.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:52.665
Why would they not take
advantage of that resource?

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.435
NARRATOR: And now, new
research suggests this has

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.502
been happening for millennia.

